    "['set mpls-label', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'then label', 'parent_command': ['set protocols ospf import [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set protocols ospf import [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `set mpls-label` command within a route-map context:\\n\\n##\\nthen label\\n##\\n\\nIn Juniper's policy-statement (equivalent to Cisco's route-map), the `then label` action is used to push an MPLS label, which corresponds to Cisco's `set mpls-label` command. This would typically be used in a routing policy like:\\n```\\npolicy-statement policy1 {\\n    term 10 {\\n        from {\\n            route-filter 0.0.0.0/0 upto /32;\\n        }\\n        then {\\n            label;\\n            accept;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n```\", 'trans_template': 'then label'}]]": 1,
    "['ip prefix-list [name] seq [sequence_number] [action] [network]/[prefix_length] le [max_prefix_length]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set policy-options prefix-list ALL 0.0.0.0/0 upto /32', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set policy-options prefix-list ALL 0.0.0.0/0 upto /32', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset policy-options prefix-list ALL 0.0.0.0/0 upto /32\\n##\\n\\nThe Juniper equivalent:\\n- \"ip prefix-list\" becomes \"policy-options prefix-list\" in Junos\\n- \"seq 5\" is omitted as Juniper automatically sequences entries\\n- \"permit\" is implied in Juniper prefix-lists (they\\'re permit-only by default)\\n- \"0.0.0.0/0 le 32\" translates to \"0.0.0.0/0 upto /32\" which means any prefix from /0 to /32\\n- Juniper uses \"set\" style configuration rather than Cisco\\'s flat commands', 'trans_template': 'set policy-options prefix-list ALL 0.0.0.0/0 upto /32'}]]": 1
